<header><p class="st-kicker">nomadic confederation · Poland, Lithuania and Russia</p><h1 class="st-title">Scythia</h1><p class="st-meta">700 BCE – 250 BCE (approx.) · capital Kamianka</p></header>
<p class="st-teaser">The kingdom of the Royal Scythians, Iranian horse nomads who ruled the Pontic steppe and left lavish gold-filled burial mounds.</p>
<div class="st-body"><div class="st-text"><h2>Origins</h2><p>Scythian groups moved west from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/central-asia">Central Asia</a> onto the steppe north of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/black-sea">Black Sea</a> in the 8th and 7th centuries BCE, displacing or absorbing the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/cimmerians">Cimmerians</a>. After raids deep into the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/near-east">Near East</a>, they settled into a confederation led by the Royal <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/scythians">Scythians</a> on the lower <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dnieper">Dnieper</a>, with the vast fortified site at Kamianka serving as a centre from about 400 BCE.</p><h2>Rule and society</h2><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/herodotus">Herodotus</a> describes kings ruling over subject tribes of nomads and farmers, oaths sealed in blood and horse sacrifices at royal funerals. Wealth came from tribute and from grain, slaves and hides sold to Greek colonies such as <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/olbia">Olbia Pontica</a>. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kurgan">Kurgans</a> like <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/solokha">Solokha</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/chertomlyk">Chertomlyk</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tovsta-mohyla">Tovsta Mohyla</a>, with its gold pectoral, show Greek craftsmen working to Scythian taste.</p><h2>Decline and legacy</h2><p>In about 513 BCE the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/scythians">Scythians</a> wore down the invading army of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/darius-i">Darius I</a> by retreating across the steppe. King <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ateas">Ateas</a> extended his power to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/danube">Danube</a> but died fighting <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/philip-ii-of-macedon">Philip II of Macedon</a> in 339 BCE. In the 3rd century BCE Sarmatian pressure collapsed the steppe kingdom, and Scythian rule survived only in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/crimea">Crimea</a>.</p></div><aside class="st-side"><figure class="st-fig"><img src="/images/states/scythia.jpg" alt="Scythia" width="320" height="239"><figcaption>Image: Antiquistik · CC BY-SA 4.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Scythian_kingdom_in_the_Pontic_steppe.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">source</a></figcaption></figure><dl class="st-facts"><dt>Years</dt><dd>700 BCE – 250 BCE (approx.)</dd><dt>Centuries</dt><dd><a href="/centuries/8th-century-bce">8th century BCE</a> – <a href="/centuries/3rd-century-bce">3rd century BCE</a></dd><dt>Type</dt><dd>nomadic confederation</dd><dt>Character</dt><dd>nomadic</dd><dt>Capital</dt><dd>Kamianka</dd><dt>Rulers</dt><dd>Idanthyrsus, Scyles, Ateas</dd><dt>Religion</dt><dd>Scythian polytheism</dd><dt>Language</dt><dd>Scythian</dd><dt>Today</dt><dd><a href="/countries/ukraine">Ukraine</a>, <a href="/countries/russia">Russia</a></dd><dt>Also</dt><dd>Scythian Kingdom, Royal Scythians, Pontic Scythia</dd></dl><figure class="st-locator"><a href="/atlas?year=-475&amp;focus=scythia" aria-label="Scythia on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="5493 590 640 512" role="img" aria-label="Map: where Scythia lay"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-tie" d="M5813 846L5813 846M5813 846L5750 793"/><path class="map-dot-n" d="M5813 846h0M5750 793h0"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M5813 846h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M5813 846h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks its recorded location. <a href="/atlas?year=-475&amp;focus=scythia">On the atlas &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></aside></div>
